Leave the default TLS version bounds to the system crypto policy.

SetupSsl sets the session's minimum and maximum TLS versions on every
connection (FTPS, WebDAV, S3 and the HTTP requests core makes), defaults
included: TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3. On a distribution with a system-wide crypto
policy (Fedora's crypto-policies, for one), the system OpenSSL already
applies the policy's protocol range to each new SSL_CTX through its
openssl.cnf, and a per-connection SSL_set_min_proto_version replaces that:
a policy that asks for TLS 1.3 only would be loosened to TLS 1.2, and a
LEGACY one tightened. Built with TLS_SYSTEM_POLICY defined (a build against
the system's OpenSSL), a bound is set only when the session changes it from
its default, so the defaults are the policy's and a version the user picks
still applies. The cipher lists were never set, so they are the policy's
already. Without TLS_SYSTEM_POLICY, as in upstream's build and every
Windows build, nothing changes. Line counts are unchanged.

--- a/source/core/NeonIntf.cpp
+++ b/source/core/NeonIntf.cpp
@@ -598,12 +598,12 @@
   // With Neon, we could use ne_ssl_set_protovers, but we share this with FTP
   int MinVersion = TlsVersionToOpenssl(MinTlsVersion);
-  SSL_set_min_proto_version(Ssl, MinVersion);
   int MaxVersion = TlsVersionToOpenssl(MaxTlsVersion);
-  SSL_set_max_proto_version(Ssl, MaxVersion);
-
-  // Since OpenSSL 3, SSL 3.0, TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are enabled on security level 0 only
-  if (MinVersion <= TLS1_1_VERSION)
-  {
-    SSL_set_security_level(Ssl, 0);
-  }
+#ifdef TLS_SYSTEM_POLICY // linscp: the system crypto policy's bounds, unless the session sets its own
+  if (MinTlsVersion != tlsDefaultMin) { SSL_set_min_proto_version(Ssl, MinVersion); } if (MaxTlsVersion != tlsMax) { SSL_set_max_proto_version(Ssl, MaxVersion); }
+#else
+  SSL_set_min_proto_version(Ssl, MinVersion); SSL_set_max_proto_version(Ssl, MaxVersion);
+#endif
+  // Since OpenSSL 3, SSL 3.0, TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are enabled on security level 0 only
+  if (MinVersion <= TLS1_1_VERSION) { SSL_set_security_level(Ssl, 0); }
+
 }
